Upstream's other text layout for the talker: the text read one token per frame.

modeling_qwen3_tts.py builds the non ICL prompt two ways, switched by
non_streaming_mode. True puts the whole utterance in the prefill, summed with
codec_pad, and adds only tts_pad during generation. False ends the prefill on
the first text token summed with codec_bos, and adds the rest, then tts_eos,
one row per generated frame. qwentts.cpp builds only the first. Upstream's
generate_voice_clone defaults to the second -- the one its docstring says
"simulates streaming text input" -- so the voice this project uses, Base with
a speaker vector, was being read in the layout upstream does not default to
for it.

The second layout is also the only way a reply could be read as it is
written, in one generation rather than piece by piece: the text already
arrives frame by frame, so appending to it while generating is the next
step. This patch does not do that. It adds the layout, off by default, so it
can be listened to first.

qt_tts_params.streaming_text, appended at the tail, and QT_ABI_VERSION 5 -> 6
as the header's own rule asks; v5 callers are still accepted and read as off.
QWEN_TTS_STREAMING_TEXT marks a patched header, so a caller can test for the
field without comparing ABI numbers. ICL ignores the flag: its layout is
already the streaming one.

The same layout ends worse. Its tts_eos is read at frame N_text, seconds
before the speech is over, and from there the talker decides alone when to
stop: on the weather passage, three seeds of three stopped mid vowel, the
last 20 ms at -38 dBFS with no silence after. qt_tts_params.eos_hold_frames
keeps it going: from the first draw of codec_eos, eos is masked for that many
frames and each is drawn from what remains, the first one redrawn. Two frames
(160 ms) brought all three down to -69 to -88 dBFS with 0.14 to 0.30 s of
silence; four sounded the same. 0, the default, stops at the first eos as
upstream does. QWEN_TTS_EOS_HOLD marks it.

Held past its stop, the talker usually stops again within a few frames --
the longest measured was six past the first eos -- and once did not: 62
frames, five seconds of coughing after a thirteen character sentence, on
the desktop's GPU. So at three times the hold past the first eos the
utterance ends regardless -- and what came after the hold is dropped,
because the talker made it after declining to stop, and the one time the
ceiling was hit on the desktop it was heard as a faint noise at the end.
Dropping needs the frames not to have gone out yet, so a single
streaming lane keeps its frames past the first eos back and sends them
when it finishes; a natural stop sends all of them, byte for byte what
it sent before. Several lanes flush in lockstep and cannot wait, so
there the ceiling only stops.

And the step the layout was added for: qt_tts_params.text_pull, a callback
the talker pulls the text through as it reaches it, so one generation can
follow a writer instead of the text being cut into pieces that each start
from the top. A frame whose text token has not been written yet waits for
it -- the callback blocks -- rather than being handed a tts_pad it only
ever saw after tts_eos, so the talker is given exactly what it would have
been given all at once. Tokens are committed a pre-tokenizer chunk at a
time with the last chunk held back, since more text can extend it and no
other, which makes them the whole text's tokens. On the CPU the pulled
audio matched the all-at-once audio sample for sample. The writer has to
stay above 12.5 tokens a second, one a frame at playback rate, or the
audio comes out slower than it plays. QWEN_TTS_TEXT_PULL marks it.

